# A WORST CASE for ToJava's `letIn` encoding: a hot loop that is almost
# entirely `let`s (ExampleLetChain.letChainProgram in the Arend project).
#
#   letChainInner j acc = if j == 0 then acc else
#     let a1 = acc + 1 in .. let a20 = a19 + 1 in letChainInner (j - 1) a20
#   letChainOuter i acc = if i == 0 then acc else
#     letChainOuter (i - 1) (letChainInner 10000 acc)
#   res = letChainOuter 2000 0
#
# so the value is 20 * 10000 * 2000 and 400,000,000 lets are evaluated with
# nothing between them but one `add` each.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. `letIn` can compile to a `final Object l = v;` statement, which
# costs nothing at runtime, or to λ□'s own equation `let x = v in b ==
# (\lam x => b) v`, which costs a closure allocation plus an interface dispatch.
# NO program in the corpus could separate those: `matmul`/`matmul250` contain no
# `letIn` at all (both encodings generate byte-identical Java), and the Lean
# programs mix lets with everything else. This one isolates the difference.
#
# READ IT AS A CEILING, NOT AS A COST. Being a worst case is the point. The lets
# ARE the loop body here, whereas `lean-matmul` (15 lets, some hot) is the
# realistic middle and `matmul` is the zero. A future copy-propagation pass would
# also make this program much cheaper without making real code cheaper, so a
# regression here is a bound on the damage, not a prediction of it.
#
# Arend-authored: prog.ast is `ExamplePrint:letChainSexpr` serialized by
# Serialize.ard and regenerated by test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh, the same round
# trip as `peano` and `matmul`.
#
# java ONLY, and deliberately: the C and OCaml backends would need attribute
# files remapping the primitive-op axioms, and this program exists to compare two
# JAVA encodings against each other, which an interleaved A/B does without a
# cross-backend oracle. `expected` is derived from the source by hand and is the
# oracle -- 20 lets each adding 1, per inner iteration, is a product.
expected=400000000
backends=java
runtime=
xfail=
note=letIn worst case: 400M lets in a hot loop; a CEILING on let-encoding cost, not a typical one
